<chapter xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude"  xml:id="chap_differential-equations_chap_differential-equations">
  <title>Differential Equations</title>
  <introduction>
    <p>
      The rate equations with which we began our study of calculus are called
      <term>differential equations</term>
          <idx><h>differential equation</h></idx>
      when we identify the rates of change that appear within them as derivatives of functions.
      Differential equations are essential tools in many area of mathematics and the sciences.
      In this chapter we explore three of their important uses:
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            <term>Modelling</term> problems using differential equations;
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <term>Solving</term> differential equations,
            both through numerical techniques like Euler's method and,
            where possible,
            through finding formulas which make the equations true;
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <term>Defining</term> new functions by differential equations.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
    <p>
      We also introduce two important functions<mdash/>the
      <term>exponential function</term>
      and the <term>logarithmic function</term><mdash/>which play central roles in the theory of solving differential equations.
      Finally, we introduce the operation of
      <term>antidifferentiation</term>
      as an important tool for solving some special kinds of differential equations.
    </p>
  </introduction>
